description:
The compileSwitchStmt function in compiler.ts separates value cases from the default case and always emits the default case AFTER all value cases, regardless of its position in the source.

For switch statements where default appears in the middle (which Flash AS2 allows), the fall-through semantics are wrong:

  switch(x) {
    case 1: trace('one'); break;
    default: trace('default');  // no break — should fall through to case 2
    case 2: trace('two'); break;
  }

When x=3, correct AS2 behavior: trace('default') then trace('two').
Current compiler output: trace('default') only (default is placed after case 2).

Root cause: lines 1003-1005 in compiler.ts:
  const defaultCase = stmt.cases.find((c) => c.test === null) ?? null;
  const valueCases  = stmt.cases.filter((c) => c.test !== null);
This discards the original case ordering, so fall-through from default to subsequent value cases is lost.

Fix: respect the original case order by interleaving the default case with the value cases during code generation, rather than always appending it at the end.

Note: this edge case (default in middle) is uncommon in practice but valid AS2 code. Low-medium priority.
